WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.440
Whether they feature a deal with&nbsp;
the Devil or a two-faced scientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.700
these classic horror movies don't&nbsp;
need dialogue to keep you up at night.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:16.301
"It's the Golem of Prague. Legendary&nbsp;
defender of the Jewish people!"

00:00:16.301 --> 00:00:21.300
The concept of the golem has been a popular&nbsp;
part of Jewish folklore for hundreds of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.340
and it still endures today. The legend that&nbsp;
has proven to be the most widespread centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.280
around Judah Loew ben Bezalel, a 16th-century&nbsp;
Rabbi who resided in the city of Prague. Legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.540
has it that Rabbi Loew created the golem to&nbsp;
protect the Prague ghetto from persecution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.460
This is the tale presented in the 1920&nbsp;
silent horror film called "The Golem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.980
How He Came into the World." The film itself was&nbsp;
actually the third in a trilogy, serving as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.500
prequel for a larger narrative — but the other two&nbsp;
have been lost to time. Sadly, "How He Came into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.240
the World" is the only one of Paul Wegener's&nbsp;
golem stories that you can still watch today.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.000
Just as it's described in the legend, Rabbi Loew,&nbsp;
predicting tragedy for the people of Prague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.040
looks to craft a way to protect his people. He&nbsp;
sculpts the titular golem out of humble clay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.720
and gives him life so he can fulfill his&nbsp;
role as a protector. "The Golem" holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.340
an important place in film history, given&nbsp;
its status as the earliest feature-length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:24.000
film to feature a man-made monster — over a&nbsp;
decade before "Frankenstein" came out in 1931.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.000
It's impossible to talk about silent horror&nbsp;
cinema without mentioning the German expressionism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.240
movement of the early 1900s. The style — most&nbsp;
prevalent from the 1910s to the 1930s — had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.940
an emphasis on representing feelings more so&nbsp;
than reality. This was often expressed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.460
painted dreamlike visuals which utilized&nbsp;
heavily distorted shadows and bizarre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.320
angular shapes. The German expressionist&nbsp;
masterpiece "The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.360
has been hailed by many film historians&nbsp;
as one of the first true horror films.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.640
Our protagonist is Francis, who regales a&nbsp;
stranger with the tale of an ordeal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.960
he and his fiancée Jane had faced. He&nbsp;
tells the story of how he witnessed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.980
bizarre public spectacle courtesy of&nbsp;
a mysterious doctor by the name of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.560
Caligari. The sinister doctor presents a gaunt&nbsp;
somnambulist — or sleepwalker — named Cesare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.000
who can seemingly predict the future&nbsp;
at Caligari's command. It's soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.880
revealed that Cesare is a murderous monster under&nbsp;
Caligari's control — and Jane is his next target.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.200
Everything in this world, from the&nbsp;
windows to the streets to the shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.900
has a surreal and distorted quality to&nbsp;
it. Despite being over 100 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:43.308
"The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari" is still an&nbsp;
effectively atmospheric and unsettling watch.

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:51.668
"Now, you all know the plot to 'Jekyll&nbsp;
and Hyde'… but you don't know the story."

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:58.080
Robert Louis Stevenson's 1886 novella "The&nbsp;
Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.180
often regarded as a seminal work of horror&nbsp;
fiction. Its influence has wormed its way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.460
into countless other works — from comedies&nbsp;
like "Me, Myself, and Irene" to comic books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.320
like "The Incredible Hulk." The story of&nbsp;
Jekyll and his sinister alter ego Hyde has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.180
been put to screen a number of times — but one&nbsp;
of the most famous takes on the iconic story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.640
is the production released in 1920, starring&nbsp;
John Barrymore as the dueling personalities.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.660
While this version isn't a fully accurate&nbsp;
adaptation of the original story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:34.140
its terrifying take on Mr. Hyde more than makes&nbsp;
up for it. John Barrymore's portrayal is nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:39.780
short of disturbing, aided by his naturally&nbsp;
expressive face working in tandem with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.420
mild makeup enhancements. It was so disturbing at&nbsp;
the time that, according to an archived review of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.680
the film in Photoplay Magazine, many were&nbsp;
worried that his performance would prove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.560
unhealthy for expecting mothers. There have been&nbsp;
many other versions of Stevenson's haunting tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.622
of science gone wrong, but this early silent&nbsp;
version still stands out as one of the finest.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:08.880
This next selection of silent screams comes from&nbsp;
Sweden in the early 1920s. "The Phantom Carriage,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.160
directed by Victor Sjöström, is based on the 1912&nbsp;
novel "Thy Soul Shall Bear Witness!" by Selma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.640
Lagerlöf. This eerie experience follows a boozer&nbsp;
named David Holm who, after an ill-timed fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.980
dies just as the clock strikes 12 on New&nbsp;
Year's Eve. Now he must accompany Death in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.780
his carriage and collect the souls of the dead&nbsp;
for the following year. David is also shown key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.460
moments from his life — certain key decisions&nbsp;
that he definitely could've made differently.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:40.080
At its core, "The Phantom Carriage" is a&nbsp;
fantastical morality tale that utilizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.180
horror elements to convey captivating themes&nbsp;
and ideas. It's no surprise that the film has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.040
been praised for its influence on other classic&nbsp;
movies. If you're looking for haunting visuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:55.344
and plenty of lingering questions, definitely&nbsp;
take a ride on "The Phantom Carriage."

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:01.740
"Haxan" is another example of Swedish horror and a&nbsp;
prime example of how early filmmakers would try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.720
push the envelope. This movie is a bizarre beast,&nbsp;
as it switches between fictional narrative tidbits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.300
and documentary-style segments — both focused on&nbsp;
the "facts" and folklore concerning witchcraft.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.080
The documentary segments are quite&nbsp;
slow and feature a lot of pointing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.940
but contain some interesting facts regarding&nbsp;
witches and Satan worship. These pair nicely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.360
with the narrative portions, which&nbsp;
feature dramatizations containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.340
horrific visuals that still hold up today.&nbsp;
These include grotesque looking demons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.360
even a depiction of Satan himself, played&nbsp;
by the film's director Benjamin Christensen.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.880
To have this level of rampantly satanic&nbsp;
imagery back in the 1920s was quite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.400
artistic gamble — but it seems that the risk paid&nbsp;
off. One hundred years later, the film is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.860
regarded as a stylistic trendsetter, especially&nbsp;
through its distinctive hybrid format. Any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.663
film that uses documentary segments to further a&nbsp;
fictional narrative owes its existence to "Haxan."

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.780
"We're seeing 'Nosferatu.'"

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.400
"Yeah, I don't know why I'm going, but&nbsp;
I'm just in a weird headspace today."

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.160
"Which is exactly how you wanna&nbsp;
see German expressionism."

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:09.900
Certain horror films are more than just&nbsp;
memorable, instead achieving a level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:17.340
influence that can last a century — and definitely&nbsp;
longer. This is the case with F. W. Murnau's 1922&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.200
picture "Nosferatu," which features one&nbsp;
of the earliest onscreen depictions of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.896
vampire. A loose adaptation of Bram Stoker's&nbsp;
"Dracula" — with key details changed to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.896 --> 00:06:31.560
any legal conflict with Stoker's widow&nbsp;
— the handsome vampire was turned into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:37.500
a wretched ghoul called Count Orlok, played by&nbsp;
Max Schreck. "Nosferatu" follows Thomas Hutter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.660
who travels to Transylvania to secure the&nbsp;
signature of Count Orlok for a real estate deal.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:47.940
Soon enough, Hutter's wife finds herself in&nbsp;
Orlok's crosshairs, meaning that Hutter must race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:54.420
against the clock to save his beloved. "Nosferatu"&nbsp;
is a true chiller in every sense of the word:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.760
from Count Orlok rising from his coffin to&nbsp;
his sinister shadow being cast on the walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.500
this film is loaded with unforgettable&nbsp;
imagery. To this day, many critics still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.540
hail "Nosferatu" as a legendary classic of the&nbsp;
horror genre — and it isn't hard to see why.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:16.020
No list of silent horror classics would be truly&nbsp;
complete without an appearance or two by the man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:21.900
of a thousand faces, Lon Chaney. Chaney's&nbsp;
now-legendary physical language and facial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:27.600
expressions originated out of familial necessity,&nbsp;
as both of his birth parents were deaf. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.340
command of physical performance, along with his&nbsp;
skill with theatrical makeup, helped to propel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.500
him into the limelight. Chaney soon became best&nbsp;
known for his on-screen portrayals of physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.300
deformed, often misunderstood misfits, like&nbsp;
Quasimodo in "The Hunchback of Notre Dame."&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.860
This film, while not the first, stands&nbsp;
as one of the more famous adaptations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.980
Victor Hugo's famous 1831 novel. For such an&nbsp;
early film production, everything in it feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:58.140
grand in scale — especially the sets, which&nbsp;
all perfectly capture the film's setting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:02.940
15th century France. But of course, the major&nbsp;
highlight of the film is Chaney as Quasimodo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.860
who takes advantage of every moment he's on&nbsp;
screen. "The Hunchback of Notre Dame" is still a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.020
silent cinematic classic, and the film that helped&nbsp;
to propel Lon Chaney to silver screen stardom.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.880
Long before the Phantom of the Opera was&nbsp;
belting out melodramatic show tunes on Broadway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:23.280
he was terrifying moviegoers in 1925.&nbsp;
In another one of his iconic roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.160
Lon Chaney plays the titular Phantom, a&nbsp;
disfigured musical genius who resides in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.080
decrepit sewers below the Paris Opera House.&nbsp;
He falls madly in love with Christine Daaé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.880
an understudy turned starlet who he&nbsp;
begins puppeteering from the shadows.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:45.120
This take on the tale is truly spellbinding, from&nbsp;
its sinister musical arrangements to Chaney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.980
awe-inspiring performance — equal parts&nbsp;
horrific and heartbreaking. Underneath his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.960
twisted appearance, Chaney conveys a tangible&nbsp;
pain and rage through his horrific makeup and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.420
prosthetics. From start to finish, the film —&nbsp;
much like the Phantom himself — has you under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.980
its gothic spell. Without Rupert Julian's&nbsp;
adaptation of Gaston Leroux's story, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:10.140
of the tropes and visuals we associate with "The&nbsp;
Phantom of the Opera" probably wouldn't exist.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.080
Japan has always had an affinity for truly&nbsp;
resonant and out-of-the-box horror — even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:21.960
going back many decades. Terrifying creations&nbsp;
from the Land of the Rising Sun often reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.240
fears and paranoias that apply heavily to&nbsp;
their native cultural influences. No film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:31.920
made before World War II is a better example&nbsp;
of this than director Teinosuke Kinugasa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.500
"A Page of Madness." The 1926 movie concerns&nbsp;
a husband who has taken a janitorial job at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.000
a mental institution to remain close to&nbsp;
his institutionalized wife — but as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.380
employment continues, the lines between&nbsp;
fantasy and reality begin to erode.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.400
With its twisted angles and&nbsp;
haunting imagery involving masks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.960
"A Page of Madness" will most definitely be&nbsp;
creeping into your nightmares if you watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.240
it late at night. Kinugasa's masterpiece is&nbsp;
a one-of-a-kind experience that's definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.721
worthy of all the praise that's&nbsp;
been heaped on it over the years.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.420
"Oh, you know the 'Faust' story?"

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:07.680
"No."

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.180
Dozens of iconic characters&nbsp;
have made deals with the devil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:17.520
but none are more influential than Faust.&nbsp;
Directed by F. W. Murnau of "Nosferatu" fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.680
this adaptation of the German legend shares the&nbsp;
same expressionist style as its predecessor.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:28.380
The story of "Faust" concerns a wager between the&nbsp;
demon Mephisto and an Archangel — the demon bets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.940
the angel that he can corrupt even the most&nbsp;
righteous man's soul. They decide on Faust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.960
a man willing to dabble in infernal&nbsp;
arts in order to help others. Led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.940
to a crossroads by a mystical book, Faust,&nbsp;
Mephisto, and Lucifer come to an agreement.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:47.220
Faust tries to use his newfound abilities&nbsp;
to aid people, but his satanic affiliations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:52.140
isolate him further. As time goes&nbsp;
on, his desire for power and glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.640
corrupts him and the world around him.&nbsp;
Given its use of satanic iconography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:01.680
"Faust" is loaded with surreal and&nbsp;
chilling visuals — the most memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.860
of which features the devil towering over a&nbsp;
small village as he spreads a deadly plague.

